<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.15.4: Comparing Linear and Exponential Growth</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01512</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.15.4: Comparing Linear and Exponential Growth</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5b96daf3-b5b0-4241-8a9f-780f6c2023c0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title> Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> The linear function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x)=4x\). </item>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Show that \(g(3)\) and \(g(2)\) have a difference of 4. 
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Compare your answer:
      <list>
        <item> \(h(3) = 4^3= 64\), and \(h(2) = 4^2 = 16\). The quotient is \(\frac{64}{16}\), which is 4. </item>
        <item>\(\frac{
          h(3)}{h(2)}=\frac {4^3}{4^2}=4^{3−2} = 4\) </item>
      </list>
    </item>
    
    <item> Show that when the input value increases from \(x\) to \(x+1\), the output values \(g(x+1)\) and \(g(x)\) have a difference of 4. 
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(g(x+1) − g(x) = 4 \cdot (x+1) − 4x = 4 \cdot (x+1−x) = 4 \cdot (1)\), which equals 4.
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> The exponential function \(h\) is defined by \(h(x)=4^x\). </item>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Show that \(h(3)\) and \(h(2)\) have a quotient of 4. 
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Compare your answer:
      <list>
        <item> \(h(3) = 4^3= 64\), and \(h(2) = 4^2 = 16\). The quotient is \(\frac{64}{16}\), which is 4. </item>
        <item>\(\frac{
          h(3)}{h(2)}=\frac {4^3}{4^2}=4^{3−2} = 4\) </item>
      </list>
    </item>
    
    <item> Show that when the input value increases from \(x\) to \(x+1\), the output values \(h(x+1)\) and \(h(x)\) have a quotient of 4. 
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac {h(x+1)}{h(x)}=\frac{4^{x+1}}{4^x}=4^{x+1−x} = 4^1\), which equals 4.
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>Students will have additional opportunities to compare linear and exponential growth in Unit 6, so there is no need to slow down.</para></section>
</content>
